    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_kneser_graph - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_kneser_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_kneser_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a Kneser graph K(n, k) to the current graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_kneser_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a Kneser graph K(n, k) to the current graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_kneser_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add a Kneser graph K(n, k) to the current graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a Kneser graph K(n, k) to the current graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L10384-L10533){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_kneser_graph(\n    n,\n    k,\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Kneser graph](../assets/graphs/add-kneser-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three Kneser graphs side by side, each node labeled with its subset, joined when the subsets are disjoint. K(4, 1) is the complete graph on four singletons; K(4, 2) is three separate edges, each pair joined to its complement; K(5, 2) is the Petersen graph on the ten pairs from 0 to 4.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates one node for every `k`-element subset of `{0, 1, ..., n-1}` and joins two nodes\nwhen their subsets are disjoint. The graph has `C(n, k)` nodes (\"n choose k\"), and each\nnode has degree `C(n - k, k)`. `K(n, 1)` is the complete graph on `n` nodes, `K(5, 2)` is\nthe Petersen graph, and when `n < 2k` no two subsets are disjoint, so there are no edges.\nLovász proved that the chromatic number of `K(n, k)` is `n - 2k + 2`, a celebrated\nresult of topological combinatorics.\n\nEach node is labelled with the elements of its subset, comma-separated and in increasing\norder (e.g. `\"0,2\"`), and nodes are created in lexicographic order of their subsets. In a\ndirected graph each edge points from the earlier subset to the later one. The graph is\nappended without touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is\nrecorded as a single step. The Johnson graph (see `add_johnson_graph()`) uses the same\nnodes but joins subsets that overlap in all but one element.\n\nKneser graphs are useful for:\n\n- examples in graph coloring and combinatorics with known chromatic numbers\n- modeling conflict-free pairings: nodes are fixed-size teams drawn from a pool, and an\n  edge means two teams share no member and can play at the same time\n- test cases for highly symmetric, regular graphs\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Size of the base set `{0, 1, ..., n-1}`. Must be at least `1`.\n\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Size of each subset. Must satisfy `1 <= k <= n`.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each node is labelled with its subset as described above.\n    Because these labels don't depend on the existing nodes, adding a second labelled\n    Kneser or Johnson graph with overlapping subsets raises `ValueError` (labels are\n    unique); pass `label=False` for the second one. If `False`, nodes are created\n    without labels.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),\n    no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `n=` is less than `1` or `k=` is not between `1` and `n=`.\n:::\n\n## Complexity {.doc-complexity}\n\nEvery pair of subsets is compared, so the time grows as `C(n, k)^2`. `C(n, k)` grows very\nquickly (e.g. `C(20, 10)` is 184,756), so keep `n=` modest when `k=` is near `n / 2`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_johnson_graph`) \n\n:   Add a Johnson graph.\n\n[](`~add_named_graph`) \n\n:   Add a well-known graph by name.\n\n[](`~complement`) \n\n:   Join the pairs of nodes that aren't joined.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe Kneser graph `K(n, k)` has a node for each `k`-element subset of `{0, ..., n - 1}` and\njoins two subsets that have no element in common. `K(5, 2)` has `C(5, 2) = 10` nodes, each\njoined to the `C(3, 2) = 3` pairs disjoint from it, and it's the Petersen graph:\n\n::: {#faae45e7 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_kneser_graph(n=5, k=2)\npetersen = ec.Graph().add_named_graph(\"petersen\")\n\ngraph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.is_isomorphic(petersen)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(10, 15, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe labels are the subsets, so the neighbors of `\"0,1\"` are the pairs drawn from\n`{2, 3, 4}`:\n\n::: {#b765569a .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nsorted(graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in graph.get_neighbors(\"0,1\"))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['2,3', '2,4', '3,4']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Disjoint or overlapping\n\nThe Johnson graph `J(5, 2)` has the same nodes but joins two pairs that share one element,\nand any two different pairs either share one element or none. So `J(5, 2)` is the\ncomplement of `K(5, 2)`. Both are drawn here with the same positions, `K(5, 2)` on the left\nas the Petersen graph's star inside a pentagon and `J(5, 2)` on the right:\n\n::: {#ecfede48 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\njohnson = ec.Graph().add_johnson_graph(n=5, k=2)\nrings = [\n    [\"2,4\", \"1,4\", \"1,3\", \"0,3\", \"0,2\"],\n    [\"0,1\", \"2,3\", \"0,4\", \"1,2\", \"3,4\"],\n]\n\njohnson.is_isomorphic(graph.complement()), johnson.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(True, 30)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#d8caa7b8 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.layout_shell(nlist=rings, rotate=0).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", width=320, height=320, legend=False\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\" viewBox=\"4.8 14.8 310.4 310.4\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"4.8\" y=\"14.8\" width=\"310.4\